  "activity": {
    "id": "vsepr_activity",
    "name": "VSEPR Theory: Predicting Molecular Geometry",
    "description": "A sequential AR lab guiding students through VSEPR theory: from Lewis structures and steric number counting, through the five electron-domain geometries, the distinction between electron-domain and molecular geometry, lone pair angular asymmetry, real molecule examples, prediction challenges, and a final recap.",
    "workplace": "vsepr_workplace",
    "start": "step1_intro",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step1_intro",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Introduction to VSEPR Theory",
          "description": "A central atom appears at the center of the workspace. Electron domains around it repel one another, and the molecule adopts the geometry that keeps these domains as far apart as possible. Examine the flat Lewis structure of water and recall that each single bond, double bond, triple bond, or lone pair counts as one electron domain."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_intro",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "lewis_panel",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lewis_water_flat",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "lewis_panel",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "domain_count_label",
              "poi": "default",
              "text": "Domains = Bonds + Lone Pairs"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_intro"
            },
            {
              "target": "lewis_panel",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "start"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step2_minimum_repulsion",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "The Minimum-Repulsion Principle",
          "description": "Watch two electron domains drift apart until they settle 180 degrees apart (linear). Add a third domain and observe the trigonal planar arrangement at 120 degrees, then a fourth producing a tetrahedron at 109.5 degrees. Faint repulsion vectors appear and fade as each geometry stabilizes."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "repulsion_animation",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "linear_geometry_anim",
              "poi": "domain_slot_1"
            },
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "trigonal_planar_anim",
              "poi": "domain_slot_2"
            },
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "tetrahedral_anim",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "start"
          },
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "marker_central_atom",
            "duration": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step3_full_geometry_catalog",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Completing the Five Electron-Domain Geometries",
          "description": "A fifth domain rearranges the tetrahedron into a trigonal bipyramidal shape (equatorial 120 degrees, axial 90 degrees). A sixth produces an octahedral arrangement with all neighbors at 90 degrees. Rotate the molecule freely, then review the summary panel showing all five geometries with domain counts (2,3,4,5,6) and angles (180\u00b0, 120\u00b0, 109.5\u00b0, 90\u00b0/120\u00b0, 90\u00b0)."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "trigonal_bipyramidal_anim",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "octahedral_anim",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "workbench_area",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "geometry_summary_panel",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "workbench_area",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "start"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step4_electron_vs_molecular_geometry",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Electron-Domain Geometry vs. Molecular Geometry",
          "description": "The tetrahedral arrangement returns with four bonding domains (methane, CH4) \u2014 electron-domain and molecular geometry match: both tetrahedral. Replace one bond with a lone pair (ammonia, NH3): molecular geometry becomes trigonal pyramidal while electron-domain geometry stays tetrahedral. Replace a second bond with a lone pair (water, H2O): molecular geometry becomes bent."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "methane_model",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "ammonia_model",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "water_model",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "geometry_type_label",
              "poi": "default",
              "text": "Electron-Domain: Tetrahedral | Molecular: varies"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "central_atom",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "start"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step5_lone_pair_asymmetry",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Lone Pair Angular Asymmetry",
          "description": "Compare methane, ammonia, and water side by side with labeled bond angles (109.5\u00b0, 107\u00b0, 104.5\u00b0). Lone-pair clouds appear larger than bonding domains, showing they occupy more angular space because they are held only by the central atom, compressing the remaining bond angles."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "molecule_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "methane_model",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "ammonia_model",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "water_model",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lone_pair_size_anim",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "bond_angle_label",
              "poi": "default",
              "text": "109.5\u00b0 / 107\u00b0 / 104.5\u00b0"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_asymmetry",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "molecule_set",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "start"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step6_real_molecule_gallery",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Gallery of Real Molecules",
          "description": "Explore six real molecules covering all five electron-domain geometries: CO2 (linear), BF3 (trigonal planar), CH4 (tetrahedral), PF5 (trigonal bipyramidal), SF6 (octahedral), plus lone-pair examples NH3 (trigonal pyramidal), H2O (bent), and XeF4 (square planar). View each Lewis structure alongside its 3D structure."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "molecule_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "real_molecule_gallery_video",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "molecule_set",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_gallery",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "molecule_set",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "start"
          },
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "marker_geometry_gallery",
            "duration": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step7_drag_domain_challenge",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Prediction Activity: Drag the Domains",
          "description": "Drag floating electron domains into the correct minimum-repulsion configuration. The system checks the angular arrangement and snaps domains into place within tolerance. Complete the challenge for two, three, four, five, and six domains, then for mixed configurations including lone pairs (e.g., four domains with one lone pair, forming trigonal pyramidal molecular geometry)."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "domain_drag_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "drag_domain_challenge",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "domain_drag_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "challenge_feedback_label",
              "poi": "default",
              "text": "Drag domains to minimize repulsion"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "domain_drag_kit",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "start"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step8_pick_option_challenge",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Prediction Activity: Pick the Matching Geometry",
          "description": "A Lewis structure of an unfamiliar molecule (e.g., SF4, ClF3, BrF5, or I3-) appears alongside candidate 3D structures. Determine the steric number and lone pair count, then select the matching geometry. Feedback confirms or corrects your choice, walking through the reasoning: count domains, identify electron-domain geometry, account for lone pairs, name molecular geometry."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "domain_drag_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pick_option_challenge_image",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "domain_drag_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_pick_challenge",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "domain_drag_kit",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "challenge_feedback_label",
              "poi": "default",
              "text": "Select the correct 3D structure"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
